amdxdna.neural: the Neural Kit's back end for AMD's XDNA NPU
=============================================================

The NPU of Ryzen AI processors runs designs: configurations of its array of
AI Engine tiles, compiled on a Linux host with mlir-aie and Peano. It does
not run a network's operators as such. This back end therefore takes from a
network exactly the stretches it finds a *design bundle* for, and leaves the
rest to the CPU.

The device is /dev/npu/amdxdna/0 (the amdxdna driver of the linux-drm
repository). The back end lists it when the node exists and never opens it
to find out anything: opening loads firmware and powers the NPU up. It is
opened when the first program is made and closed when the last is deleted.
Only root can open it (without an IOMMU driver a client of the NPU reaches
all of the machine's memory); for everybody else, and whenever anything
fails, the kit computes on the CPU. A program whose command fails or times
out reports B_NEURAL_DEVICE_LOST: its stretch is the CPU's for the rest of
the session.

  NEURAL_AMDXDNA_BUNDLES=<dir>   looked into before the data directories
  NEURAL_AMDXDNA_DEBUG=1         prints, for every network asked about, its
                                 graph key and what became of it


Where bundles are
-----------------

    <data directory>/neural/amdxdna/<graph key>/

in the directory NEURAL_AMDXDNA_BUNDLES names, then the data directories in
the usual order (the user's non-packaged one first). The first directory
with the network's key that has a bundle.txt decides. When none of the
places exists, nothing is computed at all.


The graph key
-------------

32 hexadecimal digits: the first 16 bytes of the SHA-256 of the stream
below. It says what the network is made of, not which weights it has, so it
is cheap for a model of any size. Integers are 32 bits, little endian;
"string" is a length and that many bytes.

    "amdxdna-graph-1\n"
    number of operators
    for each operator, in the network's order:
        string: its name, neural_operator_name() ("CONV_2D")
        number of inputs,  then a tensor number for each (-1: not given)
        number of outputs, then a tensor number for each
        size of the parameters that follow, and the parameters: the fields
            of the operator's structure in NeuralOperators.h in their
            order; enumerations and integers 32 bits, bool one byte, float
            its 32 bits; arrays only as far as their count says. A
            convolution that is not depthwise has depth_multiplier 0.
    number of tensors the operators use
    for each of them, in the order of their numbers:
        type (neural_data_type), rank, the dimensions
        one byte: quantized
        if quantized: scale (float), zero point, number of channel scales,
            and if that is not 0: the channel axis and the scales
        one byte: constant; if so, its size in bytes as 64 bits
    number of the network's inputs,  then a tensor number for each
    number of the network's outputs, then a tensor number for each

Tensor numbers are not the network's own indices: tensors are numbered from
0 in the order the operators first mention them, each operator's inputs
before its outputs. So the key does not depend on the order a file or a
program lists its tensors in, nor on their names. (-2 stands for an input
or output of the network that no operator uses.) A network with an operator
whose parameters Bundle.cpp does not know has no key.


The weights digest
------------------

A bundle is for certain weights. 64 hexadecimal digits, the SHA-256 of:

    "amdxdna-weights-1\n"
    for each operator k = 0, 1, ... of the bundle's stretch,
    for each of its inputs j that is a constant:
        k, j, the constant's size in bytes (64 bits), its bytes

It is computed only when a bundle with the network's key was found, and
remembered per network; when a program is made it is computed again.


bundle.txt
----------

Lines of name=value; lines starting with # and names not known are skipped.

    format=1
    name=<for messages>
    graph=<graph key; the directory's name>
    operators=<first>:<count>      the stretch: operators of the network
    weights=<weights digest>
    columns=<n>                    columns of the array the design takes
    ops_per_cycle=<n>              as in the design's partition (2048)
    pdi=<file>                     the configuration: a raw PDI
    insts=<file>                   the instruction sequence (32 bit words)
    opcode=<n>                     the kernel's first argument (3)
    insts_size=words|bytes         how the kernel is told the size (words)
    timeout_ms=<n>                 for one run (5000)
    buffer=<in|out|const>:<bytes>:op<k>.<in|out><j>:<layout>

One buffer line per buffer of the design, in the order of its kernel's
arguments (at most five). "op<k>.in<j>" is input j of operator k of the
stretch, "op<k>.out<j>" its output j. "in" buffers are filled before every
run and "out" buffers read after it; "const" buffers are filled once, from
the network's weights. Every tensor that enters or leaves the stretch must
have a buffer. Layouts:

    nhwc     as the kit has the tensor, byte for byte
    hcwc8    a picture [1, H, W, C] of bytes, C a multiple of 8, as
             mlir-aie's kernels take it: [H][C / 8][W][8]
    oiio8    weights [O, 1, 1, I] of a 1x1 convolution, bytes:
             [O / 8][I / 8][8 of I][8 of O]

A bundle whose stretch, buffers or layouts do not fit the network, or whose
weights digest is another, does not apply.

make-bundle.py in the linux-drm repository (haiku/tests/xdna) makes the
bundle of mlir-aie's conv2d design and says where the design's arithmetic
and the kit's agree; amdxdna_backend_test (src/tests/kits/neural) checks the
back end with it.
